      "id": 1,
      "text": "Hazlet was originally incorporated as Raritan Township in 1848 before becoming Hazlet Township in 1967.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3,
        2
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hazlet%2C_New_Jersey",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The township’s name comes from Dr. John Hazlett, who owned an estate near the Keyport-Holmdel Turnpike.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        20,
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hazlet%2C_New_Jersey",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Hazlet hosted New Jersey’s last drive‑in movie theater, the Route 35 Drive‑In, which closed in 1991.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        22
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Hazlet%2C_New_Jersey",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "NJ Transit’s Raritan Valley Line stops at Hazlet station, connecting residents to Hoboken, Newark, and New York Penn Station.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        13,
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia",
